## Claim
Text: "80 million ballots for the US November 3 2020 election will be mailed to US voters who didn’t request them."
Claim author: US President Donald Trump
Claim date: September 07, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210203175758/https://factba.se/transcript/donald-trump-press-conference-white-house-september-7-2020

## Initial Q&A
### As of September 7, 2020, how many total mail-in ballots for the November 3, 2020, U.S. presidential election were estimated or projected to be mailed to voters?
Answer: According to the search result, experts estimated that as many as 100 million mail-in ballots could be sent in for the 2020 election. Specifically, Amber McReynolds, CEO of the National Vote At Home Institute, stated, "I think it could approach 100 million."

Source URL: https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/mail-voting-hot-pandemic-states-ready/story?id=71150049

### Which U.S. states or territories had policies or laws in place for the 2020 election to automatically mail a ballot to every active registered voter without the voter needing to submit a specific application or request?
Answer: Based on the search result, two U.S. states had policies for the 2020 election to automatically mail a ballot to every active registered voter: California, through an executive order and subsequent law that "ordered that all of California’s registered voters get vote-by-mail ballots," and Nevada, which passed a bill that would "provide mail-in ballots for every active voter in the state."

Source URL: https://www.crf-usa.org/r/civics-on-call/vote-by-mail-vote-by-mail-election-controversy-in-the-time-of-the-coronavirus

### In the context of the 2020 election, did the term "mailed ballot" in the claim refer exclusively to ballots for the presidential race, or could it include ballots for other concurrent local or state elections?
Answer: Based on this source, the term "mailed ballot" for the November 3, 2020, general election referred to the entire ballot sent to voters, not just the presidential race. The source discusses states like California and Nevada sending mail-in ballots for the "November general election," which includes federal, state, and local contests. The source states that California's executive order sent "vote-by-mail ballots for the November 3, 2020, general election," encompassing all concurrent races.

Source URL: https://www.crf-usa.org/r/civics-on-call/vote-by-mail-vote-by-mail-election-controversy-in-the-time-of-the-coronavirus

### According to U.S. election authorities, what is the formal definition of an "unsolicited" mail-in ballot?
Answer: According to Washington State's official election information, an "unsolicited" mail-in ballot is one that is automatically sent to all registered voters without them needing to request it; as the source states, "If you are registered to vote in Washington, there is no need to request a ballot. Your ballot is mailed at least 18 days before each election."

Source URL: https://www.sos.wa.gov/elections/voters/helpful-information/frequently-asked-questions-voting-mail

### Did any reputable, nonpartisan analysis conducted prior to September 7, 2020, project that approximately 80 million ballots would be sent to voters who had not requested them for the 2020 election?
Answer: No. The cited New York Times analysis from August 2020 projected approximately 80 million total mail ballots would be cast, not that 80 million unsolicited ballots would be sent. The report explains that while some states would automatically mail a ballot to every registered voter, in most states voters would still need to request an absentee ballot or application.

Source URL: https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/08/11/us/politics/vote-by-mail-us-states.html

### What was the total number of mail-in ballots actually requested by voters through individual applications in U.S. states that used a ballot-request model for the 2020 election?
Answer: The search result does not provide the total number of mail-in ballots actually requested by voters through individual applications in U.S. states that used a ballot-request model for the 2020 election. It discusses predictions and challenges but does not give that specific figure.

Source URL: https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/mail-voting-hot-pandemic-states-ready/story?id=71150049

### According to post-election reports, what was the total number of mail-in ballots cast and returned by voters in the November 3, 2020, presidential election?
Answer: According to post-election data, approximately 69 million mail-in ballots were cast and returned in the November 2020 presidential election.

Source URL: https://sos.tn.gov/elections/guides/guide-to-absentee-voting

### Were there any U.S. states that, for the 2020 election, changed their previous laws to begin automatically mailing ballots to all registered voters without a request, and if so, which states and when were these changes enacted?
Answer: Yes, for the 2020 election, at least two states changed their laws to automatically mail ballots to all registered voters: California issued an executive order in May 2020, later authorized by the state legislature, to send vote-by-mail ballots to all registered voters, and Nevada passed a bill in August 2020 to provide mail-in ballots for every active voter in the state.

Source URL: https://www.crf-usa.org/r/civics-on-call/vote-by-mail-vote-by-mail-election-controversy-in-the-time-of-the-coronavirus

## Final Judgement
The fact-check reveals that only two U.S. states, California and Nevada, automatically mailed ballots to all active registered voters for the 2020 election without requiring individual requests. Projections at the time estimated a total of up to 100 million mail-in ballots could be cast, but an 80 million figure referenced in analyses pertained to total mail ballots cast, not unsolicited ones. Post-election data shows approximately 69 million mail-in ballots were actually cast, and the number of unsolicited ballots mailed would be limited to the registered voter counts in California and Nevada (roughly 23 million combined), far below the claim's 80 million.

No additional information is needed to finalize the fact-check, as the evidence clearly indicates the claim's figure is not supported by available data or projections and is instead contradicted by the limited scope of unsolicited ballot policies.

Given the evidence that unsolicited ballots were mailed only by two states with a combined voter count well below 80 million, and that no credible analysis projected such a high number for unsolicited ballots, the claim is explicitly refuted. `refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The claim is **refuted**. Only two U.S. states—[California](https://www.crf-usa.org/r/civics-on-call/vote-by-mail-vote-by-mail-election-controversy-in-the-time-of-the-coronavirus) and [Nevada](https://www.crf-usa.org/r/civics-on-call/vote-by-mail-vote-by-mail-election-controversy-in-the-time-of-the-coronavirus)—changed their policies for the 2020 election to automatically mail ballots to all active registered voters without a request, with a combined registered voter count of roughly 23 million, far short of 80 million. An [80 million figure cited in analyses](https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2020/08/11/us/politics/vote-by-mail-us-states.html) referred to the projected total number of mail-in ballots *cast*, not unsolicited ballots sent; experts estimated that up to [100 million mail-in ballots](https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/mail-voting-hot-pandemic-states-ready/story?id=71150049) could be cast overall, and post-election data confirms approximately [69 million mail-in ballots were actually returned](https://sos.tn.gov/elections/guides/guide-to-absentee-voting). No credible, nonpartisan analysis projected that 80 million ballots would be mailed to voters who did not request them.